stion that they bring wouldn't it be  better if we could have better relations  with Russia and the answer to that is  obviously yes it would be great if we  could have a better relationship with  Russia the problem being Russia has  never demonstrated that it would like to  have a better relationship with us  particularly with the United States of  America which it still declares as its  primary enemy in the world and until we  accept that language and hold them  accountable for what they're doing to  our own country first and foremost the  fact that there will be no consequences  for Russia for what they did to American  society in our election is absolutely  unconscionable and it can a complete  abdication of responsibility from the  president  so that alone is bad enough but it's not  just that it's everything else  everywhere Russia is sort of advancing  its interests through military means  through informational means through  intelligence operations through  financial and political operations where  they are not held accountable for  directly disrupting the interests of  other nations they will keep doing these  things and and they will keep using  these sort of shadowy tactics and shadow  warfare to achieve their interests in  the world and that is directly to our  disadvantage and to the disadvantage of  American security and American  prosperity and a number of other things  along those lines were still learning  the the extent to which this Donald  Trump jr. meeting with a Russian lawyer  and those revelations are going to  impact the broader investigation into  Trump and Russia collusion but putting  the specifics of that aside uh just for  a second we're continuing to see some  defenders of Donald Trump who seem  convinced that regardless of whether or  not there were attempts from Russia to  influence or collude with the United  States for Donald Trump's benefit that  they've sort of gotten nothing out of it  right and I hear often that hey how  stupid was it for Russia to try to get  Trump elected because Trump's done  nothing for them nothing has improved  for Russia since Donald Trump got into  power we've got a pretty significantly  growing list of ways in which Donald  Trump's election has so far been a  pretty significant game-changer for  Russia hasn't it and this includes the  three issues that that you write about  that came out of that g20 meeting  between Trump and Russia this includes  the immediate attempts to start  rethinking sanctions and return these  two compounds in I believe it's Maryland  in New York it's quite a list absolutely  and and you know Russia has been very  clear about that the spokesman for Putin  came out after just yesterday so after  the meeting and said yeah we agreed to  all sorts of stuff and we expect  President Trump to start delivering he  hasn't yet if he doesn't we're going to  take retaliatory measures and that was  really notable and I think this is it so  they're sort of the tactical list of  items is the key thing to look at you  know are these are we making  sanctions are we giving back diplomatic  properties when they have done nothing  to be held accountable for the action  that took those away you know all these  other things on the list that people are  keeping a close eye on but near the  other part of that piece that I wrote in  Politico was about the rhetoric that  President Trump used in Poland and I  think this is actually the greatest  concession to to Putin it from this trip  and for the last well since he since he  entered the Kremlin but but certainly  for the last ten years and especially  since just before the annexation of  Crimea Putin has been aggressively  advancing this new narrative of the  world that has been sort of based on his  own decade-long revision of history and  everybody's placed in history part of  which is this sort of repetition that  that democratic governance is  essentially an anomaly in history and  moving forward it will move back to more  traditional systems but just what you've  heard from President Trump was this  really remarkable you know how do you  give a speech in Poland without  mentioning democracy and it's virtually  impossible for an American president to  do that and yet he did he didn't talk  about our alliances he talked about  sovereignty which is exactly how Russia  talks about itself he didn't talk about  values he talked about this idea of  civilization which absent the concept of  Western values means a very different  thing that is anchored in a  thousand-year-old history and sort of  has a more ethno-religious identity it  has very little to do with the  post-world War - Liberal Democratic  World Order  he didn't talk about rights he talked  about traditional values and identity  and these ideas of Destiny and all of  these things sort of linked to this  liberal worldview that handful of  leaders many of whom Trump has given  significant time to including Putin  including the leader of Poland including  people like Viktor Orban and Hungary the  leader in the Philippines but these  people who espouse this new liberal  worldview this idea that the liberal  democracy small-l liberal democracy but  but the concept of what has given the  United States its greatest security and  prosperity in history it's somehow  something that needs to be undone and  hearing the American president repeat  those ideas and sort of align the u.s.  behind them during  speech in Poland it's very significant I  think we're very slow to hear what he's  actually telling us this was not a new  speech it was the continuation of his  American carnage inauguration speech but  on an international stage and until we  hear what he is telling us which is that  he does not believe in the core values  that we believe are essential to our  identity in the world he is telling his  core supporters in the United States  these are things that are no longer  important and those ideas are starting  to spread and people are starting to  echo them and this is really going to  change how our allies view us how we are  positioned in the world and our own it  comes to to be secure and to access  markets and to grow our economy this is  going to have really significant impact  on the United States of America well  that's why that's why Molly I'm  concerned when I see we've got some  pretty interesting recent polling about  how significantly the reputation of the  US as a trustworthy Ally is the place to  go when you need to talk about whether  it be a technology to fight climate  change or whatever the case may be the  reputation of the US has already been  pretty significantly damaged under  Donald Trump and I feel as though there  are many Trump supporters who see this  as sort of validation like if other  countries don't like what we're doing it  must be because it's so good for us and  bad for them but it sort of misses that  this is not good the us standing being  affected in this way around the world  it's bad for national security it's bad  for economics it's bad for education  technology but this is going to affect  people whether or not they realize it  isn't it absolutely I think part of this  is that our leaders have done a very bad  job explaining this for a really long  time and that matters and you know part  of the reason that it's been so easy to  give the hardcore Trump supporters these  very quick talking points that they've  echoed about NATO being worthless and  everybody being out to get us and  everything's unfair and trade is unfair  is that nobody really explains what has  been important about the last 70 years  of our history I think this notion that  Europe is a free rider and it's just  sort of been mooching off the US since  the Marshall Plan and we've been doing  everything for them and it doesn't  matter to us and we should stop is  really really off base and I mean the  yes we invested a ton of money in  European economy and security but what  that did was bill  a market for our goods it moved us from  being a significant global economy to  being a global superpower economy it  moved us from being a significant global  power to being you know the superpower  in terms of military and power in the  world and all of that was because we  built a market and we built an alliance  that that amplified our power and our  reach into the world and that's really  significant and we have to understand  what that means  we've been speaking with Molly McHugh  foreign policy and strategy consultant  information warfare expert the article  is in Politico Trump handed Putin a  stunning victory Molly great is always  to talk to you thanks so much for having  me on

